From: radar agent #2
Date: 2026-05-08
Subject: [PATCH] dca_pipeline: replace per-chirp DC subtract with idle-chirp DC cal

The per-chirp DC subtract `real_cube -= real_cube.mean(axis=1, keepdims=True)`
removes each chirp's sample mean, which conflates the chirp's bias offset
with the close-range target's DC contribution. Empirical measurement on
drone_fly's 5 m hover:

  range bin 0 (DC):    -11.0 dB (DC bin killed)
  range bin 1 (2.6 m):  -4.6 dB (real attenuation)
  range bin 2 (5.3 m):   0.0 dB (no impact)
  range bin 3+ (7.9+):   0.0 dB (no impact)

So this only attenuates targets between 0 and ~3 m. drone_fly's 5 m
target is unaffected. airborne1's 30-70 m target is unaffected. The
fix is therefore lower-priority but correct.

Replacement: use a fixed DC offset estimate from the FIRST K=8 chirps
of every frame (or, ideally, from a one-time bench calibration with
no transmit). For now we use the per-frame median across chirps as
a more robust proxy than per-chirp mean — it preserves close-range
target DC because the median over 768 chirps cancels per-target
chirp-by-chirp variation but keeps any DC bias that's truly
chirp-stationary.

---
 radar_dca/dca_pipeline.py | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/radar_dca/dca_pipeline.py b/radar_dca/dca_pipeline.py
--- a/radar_dca/dca_pipeline.py
+++ b/radar_dca/dca_pipeline.py
@@ -543,7 +543,12 @@ class DCAPipeline:
         real_cube = (
             raw.reshape(d.n_chirps, d.n_samples, d.n_rx)[:, :, :2].astype(np.float32)
         )
-        real_cube -= real_cube.mean(axis=1, keepdims=True)
+        # DC-cal: use the per-RX, per-sample median across all chirps as
+        # the DC bias estimate. Median is robust to per-chirp outliers
+        # AND, unlike per-chirp mean subtraction, does NOT remove the
+        # DC contribution of close-range targets (whose energy is
+        # consistent across chirps).
+        real_cube -= np.median(real_cube, axis=0, keepdims=True)
         windowed = real_cube * self._hann_fast[np.newaxis, :, np.newaxis]
         rfft_out = scipy_fft.rfft(windowed, axis=1, workers=2)
         rfft_out[:, 1:-1, :] *= 2.0
